The PL/SQL lexer and parser grammars derive from the project's pinned
antlr/grammars-v4 source, commit 7df52be94698550d219d299d04105c6bafadd9c3.
Original grammar copyright headers and Apache-2.0 terms are preserved in
grammar/ and LICENSE-GRAMMAR-APACHE-2.0.txt.
The grammar-derived recognizers retain those grammar copyright and Apache-2.0
terms. Project-authored adapter and hook code follows the repository license.

The native adaptations rename project-authored helper callbacks and express
the existing combined last-unit/slash predicate as one Rust hook. The hooks
preserve the retained Java adapter's behavior; they do not certify Oracle
server compatibility, target effects, approval or production qualification.

The committed Rust recognizers are generated with antlr-rust-codegen 0.34.0
and use antlr-rust-runtime 0.34.0 (BSD-3-Clause). Both projects' dependency
archives retain their own copyright and license notices. No Java generator,
JVM or RustPython interpreter is needed by this crate at build or runtime.
Regeneration is an explicit separate developer operation.

Run `cargo run --locked -p kelryvo-oracle-grammar --example regenerate --
/absolute/path/to/antlr4-rust-gen --check` to reproduce the pinned sources.
Use --write only when intentionally updating generated files and provenance.
The generator executable must report version 0.34.0. Regeneration verifies the
original grammar hashes against docs/dependencies/antlr-parser-lock.json and
requires every semantic coordinate to be handled. A guarded mechanical repair
renames the generated grammar metadata static to GRAMMAR_METADATA because the
Oracle METADATA token otherwise collides with it; token identities are unchanged.
grammar/provenance.json records adapted and generated source hashes. This does
not change historical parser profiles, approval envelopes or Oracle qualification.
